\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

On multiple-choice benchmarks, item selection drives 34--38\% of evaluation variance while prompt wording is negligible ($<$3\%). On the free-form reasoning task GSM8K, the structure shifts: model$\times$prompt interaction alone accounts for approximately 9\%, while item selection drops to 17\%. These numbers, drawn from our factorial analysis of 2.3 million evaluation records across five benchmarks, reveal that the variance structure of LLM evaluation is not a fixed property of the pipeline but shifts qualitatively with the response format. Current evaluation practice treats variance sources as independent nuisance factors and controls them one at a time: one study perturbs prompts, another varies precision, a third changes item subsets. This piecemeal approach cannot capture interactions between sources, nor can it predict how the relative importance of each source changes across evaluation settings. Leaderboard rankings built on single-condition evaluations are therefore sensitive to methodological choices that remain unreported and uncontrolled \citep{rodriguez2021leaderboards, dehghani2021benchmark}. No unified factorial framework exists to decompose these sources simultaneously, quantify their interactions, and compare the resulting variance structure across benchmarks.

Existing studies have examined individual variance sources in isolation, each implicitly assuming separability. \citet{sclar2024formatting} documented up to 76\% accuracy swings from prompt formatting perturbations but held hardware and item selection fixed. BrittleBench \citep{brittlebench2026} found 50\% variance from prompt perturbation under the same constraint. \citet{yuan2025layercast} showed that numerical precision and GPU type cause up to 9\% accuracy variation but provided no factorial attribution to specific sources or their interactions. \citet{mesting2026hidden} applied Generalizability theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation and decomposed variance across five methodology facets on a single benchmark; judge identity dominated at 43.8\%, but the study included no cross-benchmark comparison and excluded infrastructure facets such as numerical precision. Studies of answer ordering \citep{pezeshkpour2024ordering} and hyperparameter sensitivity \citep{dodge2020finetuning, henderson2018deep} have documented additional sources of variation, again in isolation from the factors above. Item Response Theory (IRT) approaches \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks, lalor2019irt} model item difficulty parameters to construct efficient benchmarks but do not account for prompt, seed, or ordering facets. \citet{bouthillier2021accounting} established ANOVA-based variance decomposition as a principled template for ML reproducibility, but this work predates the LLM evaluation context where benchmark format introduces an additional structural variable. Three gaps remain: no study has (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology facets in a unified factorial design, (b) compared the resulting variance structure across benchmarks and response formats, or (c) translated variance components into design-dependent reproducibility budgets.

We address these gaps with a multi-benchmark G-theory study \citep{shavelson1991generalizability, brennan2001generalizability} that crosses seven facets (item, model, numerical precision, prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, and answer ordering) across eight models and five benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, GSM8K, and MATH), totaling 2.3 million records. The central finding is that variance structure is format-conditional. All three multiple-choice benchmarks share an item-dominated structure: item selection accounts for 34--38\% of total variance, with model$\times$item interaction as the second-largest source (30--36\%). Free-form benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH) exhibit a different profile: item, model, model$\times$item, and model$\times$prompt each contribute 5--24\%, with no single dominant source and larger residual variance (40--44\%). The gap between MC item dominance (34--38\%) and free-form item shares (17--24\%) reflects a systematic format-dependent pattern in what drives evaluation variance. In multiple-choice settings, the item pool determines measurement reliability; in free-form settings, model identity and model$\times$prompt interaction matter as much as item selection. A second finding is that model$\times$item interaction (up to 36\%) is the hidden second-largest variance source, larger than all methodology main effects combined. Models differ not in overall ability but in which specific items they find difficult. D-study projections translate these findings into practical design guidance: the format-conditional structure implies that reliability optimization strategies must differ between MC and free-form benchmarks. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes the contrast across all five benchmarks.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Format-conditional variance structure.} We show that the dominant variance source shifts between response formats: item selection dominates multiple-choice benchmarks (34--38\%) while item, model, model$\times$item, and model$\times$prompt contribute 5--24\% each in free-form generation. Model$\times$item interaction (up to 36\%) is the hidden second-largest source in cross-model analysis.

    \item \textbf{Precision separability.} Under binary scoring, BF16 and FP16 yield functionally identical results ($<$0.1\% interaction with all other facets). This separability is metric-conditional: text-exact-match scoring reveals a 1.3\% precision$\times$temperature interaction absent under binary scoring.

    \item \textbf{Design-dependent reproducibility budgets.} Multi-facet evaluation designs require 10.0$\times$ fewer items than single-condition designs to achieve $G \geq 0.80$ (55 versus 552 items). IRT-selected 116-item subsets yield $G = 0.457$ under single-condition protocols, which confirms that item quality and evaluation design improve reliability through orthogonal mechanisms.

    \item \textbf{Open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study calculator.} We release a variance decomposition toolkit and D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Practitioners can compute the number of items, models, and conditions needed for a target reliability before data collection.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Generalizability Theory}
\label{sec:gtheory}

Generalizability theory (G-theory) treats each measurement as one observation from a universe of admissible conditions defined by the crossed facets of an evaluation design \cite{shavelson1991generalizability, brennan2001generalizability}. The \emph{object of measurement} is the entity whose consistency is of interest. In our setting, test items ($p$) serve as the object of measurement, and the goal is to determine how reliably item-level scores generalize across the methodological conditions under which they are collected.

A \emph{G-study} decomposes total observed variance into components $\sigma^2_x$ for each facet $x$ and their interactions. Each component quantifies how much variability that source contributes to the overall measurement. Large interaction components (e.g., $\sigma^2_{pM}$ for item-by-model) indicate that facets do not affect items uniformly but instead create differential difficulty profiles. We estimate variance components via Henderson Method~I, an unbiased ANOVA-based estimator for balanced designs \cite[Ch.~3]{brennan2001generalizability}, with 1,000-resample bootstrap confidence intervals \cite{li2023bootstrap}.

A \emph{D-study} projects the generalizability coefficient under hypothetical designs with different numbers of conditions per facet:
\begin{equation}
G_\text{item} = \frac{\sigma^2_p}{\sigma^2_p + \sigma^2_\text{rel}}
\label{eq:gcoeff}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma^2_\text{rel}$ aggregates all item-by-facet interaction variances, each divided by the number of conditions for the corresponding facets \cite[Ch.~4]{brennan2001generalizability}. This prospective capability distinguishes G-theory from classical ANOVA: rather than only attributing variance post hoc, a D-study enables optimization of evaluation designs \emph{before} data collection \cite{shavelson1989gtheory}. Throughout this paper, we use the following notation: $p$ = item, $M$ = model, $\pi$ = numerical precision, $\delta$ = prompt template, $s$ = random seed, $\tau$ = sampling temperature, $o$ = answer ordering, and $\sigma^2_x$ = variance component for source $x$.

\subsection{Experimental Design: MMLU}
\label{sec:exp001}

We design a fully crossed factorial experiment (exp-001) that simultaneously varies seven facets of LLM evaluation on MMLU. Table~\ref{tab:design_matrix} summarizes the complete design matrix, and Figure~\ref{fig:design_diagram} illustrates the crossing structure.

\paragraph{Facets.} Items ($p$): 200 MMLU questions stratified across 10 subjects (20 per subject). The stratified sampling ensures that variance estimates are not dominated by a single knowledge domain; the 10 subjects span STEM, humanities, social sciences, and professional disciplines. Models ($M$): eight open-weight 7--9B parameter instruction-tuned models spanning eight architecture families, treated as a quasi-random facet (\S3.6). Numerical precision ($\pi$): BF16, FP16, and FP32, all evaluated with \texttt{enforce\_eager=True} for fair comparison across precision levels. Prompt template ($\delta$): six semantically equivalent paraphrases of the evaluation instruction \cite{sclar2024formatting}. Random seed ($s$): six fixed values (42, 123, 456, 789, 1024, 2048). Sampling temperature ($\tau$): three levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) spanning deterministic to moderately stochastic decoding. Answer ordering ($o$): four permutations of few-shot demonstration examples (for MMLU; structurally inert for other benchmarks, see \S\ref{sec:exp002}) \cite{pezeshkpour2024ordering}.

\paragraph{Sample sizes.} The cross-model BF16 balanced subset yields 460,800 records (8 models $\times$ 200 items $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 2 temperatures $\times$ 4 orderings). A single-model analysis (Llama) spans all three precisions, yielding 259,200 binary observations across 1,296 fully balanced conditions. The BF16 subset serves as the primary cross-model sample because BF16 is the standard inference precision for these architectures.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fully crossed G-theory design. Exp-001 evaluates MMLU across all facet levels; exp-002 extends to five benchmarks with reduced crossing. $^\dagger$BF16 balanced subset for cross-model analysis.}
\label{tab:design_matrix}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Facet & Levels & exp-001 & exp-002 \\
\midrule
Item ($p$) & Stratified sample & 200 & 200$\times$5 \\
Model ($M$) & 8 architecture families & 8 & 8 \\
Precision ($\pi$) & BF16 / FP16 / FP32 & 3 & 1 \\
Prompt ($\delta$) & Paraphrases & 6 & 6 \\
Seed ($s$) & Fixed set & 6 & 6 \\
Temperature ($\tau$) & 0.0 / 0.3 / 0.7 & 3 & 2 \\
Ordering ($o$) & Demo permutations & 4 & 4 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Total records} & 460,800$^\dagger$ & 2,304,000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_design_diagram}
\caption{Fully crossed G-theory design for LLM evaluation. Each observation is a binary correctness score determined by the crossing of seven facets: item ($p$), model ($M$), precision ($\pi$), prompt ($\delta$), seed ($s$), temperature ($\tau$), and ordering ($o$).}
\label{fig:design_diagram}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Extension}
\label{sec:exp002}

To test whether the variance structure observed on MMLU generalizes across evaluation formats, exp-002 extends the design to five benchmarks: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, and HellaSwag (multiple-choice), and GSM8K and MATH (free-form generation). The design crosses 8 models $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures (0.0, 0.7) $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items per benchmark, totaling 2,304,000 records. This reduced crossing fixes precision at BF16 and uses two temperature levels to concentrate statistical power on format-conditional differences. The ordering facet operates only on MMLU, where it selects among four permutations of the few-shot demonstration examples. For all other benchmarks, the prompt construction does not use the ordering parameter; ordering is structurally inert ($\sigma^2_{\text{ordering}} = 0$ by construction) and is retained solely for design balance. The two temperature levels bracket the most common evaluation configurations: deterministic ($\tau = 0.0$) and a moderate stochastic setting ($\tau = 0.7$) representative of practical deployment.


\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

All evaluations use generation-based scoring: models produce free-text responses from which correctness is determined by answer extraction, rather than by ranking answer options via token log-likelihood. This design choice follows \citet{hua2025flaw}, who show that log-likelihood scoring conflates model confidence with surface-level token biases. We report two metrics: binary correctness (primary, 1/0 per item) and text-exact-match (secondary). Binary correctness accepts any response containing the correct answer letter or numerical value after standard normalization; text-exact-match requires the generated string to match the reference answer character-for-character.

Inference runs on vLLM~0.9.2 with default settings and without batch-invariant kernels. This configuration reflects standard practitioner conditions and preserves CUDA-level nondeterminism from floating-point non-associativity as a measurable variance source \cite{messina2026background, thinkingmachines2025batch}. Few-shot prompting follows standard protocols: 5-shot for MMLU and ARC-Challenge, 4-shot for MATH, 8-shot for GSM8K, and 10-shot for HellaSwag.

\subsection{Binary Data Assumptions}
\label{sec:binary}

Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal outcome data \cite{shavelson1991generalizability}. Our binary correctness scores (0/1) violate this assumption. We establish robustness through four complementary analyses.

\paragraph{Simulation.} We generate continuous latent scores from a probit model matched to our 1,296-condition design, then dichotomize at the empirical accuracy threshold. Spearman rank correlation between binary and continuous variance component rankings is $\rho = 0.732$ (range 0.716--0.751 across accuracy levels $p \in \{0.5, 0.6, 0.7, 0.8\}$), confirming that Henderson Method~I recovers the relative ordering of variance sources despite binary response data. Henderson Method~I identifies \texttt{item\_id} as the dominant variance source in all 100 simulation replications.

\paragraph{GLMM complement.} A generalized linear mixed model (GLMM) with marginal logit link produces variance component rankings that perfectly preserve all seven grouped component orderings ($\rho = 1.0$) relative to Henderson Method~I \cite{jiang2018bayesian, tuerlinckx2006glmm}.

\paragraph{Accuracy-range stability.} The \texttt{item\_id} variance share remains stable across subsets with mean accuracy from 0.62 to 0.72 ($r = -0.048$). This stability rules out distortion from binary floor or ceiling effects at observed accuracy levels.

\paragraph{Estimator guarantee.} Henderson Method~I produces unbiased variance component estimates for balanced designs regardless of the outcome distribution \cite[Ch.~12]{brennan2001generalizability}. Our fully crossed design satisfies this balance condition.

\subsection{Model-as-Facet Justification}
\label{sec:model_facet}

Treating eight purposively selected models as a quasi-random facet requires empirical validation. We provide two forms of evidence.

D-study cross-validation: four-model subset D-study projections predicted $G = 0.893$ at eight models; the observed $G = 0.896$ confirms the projection ($\Delta = 0.003$). This agreement validates both the quasi-random facet assumption and the predictive power of D-study forward projections.

Leave-one-model-out (LOMO) stability: all eight seven-model subsets preserve \texttt{item\_id} as the dominant component and rank \texttt{model$\times$item} as the second-largest. The consistency of rankings across all subsets indicates that no single model disproportionately drives the observed variance structure.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Variance Decomposition in ML/NLP Evaluation.}
Factorial decomposition of evaluation variance has roots in the ML reproducibility literature. \citet{bouthillier2021accounting} introduced an ANOVA-based template that decomposes benchmark variance across implementation details, hyperparameters, and data splits, establishing that design choices account for a substantial share of reproducibility failures. Several subsequent studies applied similar decomposition logic to specific facets of model evaluation \citep{dodge2020finetuning, henderson2018deep, magnusson2023reproducibility}. \citet{mesting2026hidden} brought Generalizability theory into LLM evaluation, decomposing variance across five methodology facets on a single benchmark; judge identity dominated at 43.8\%, with prompt$\times$judge interaction at 12.4\%. However, this study used a single benchmark, included no infrastructure facets such as numerical precision, and fixed the judge as an LLM evaluator rather than isolating other sources. \citet{kunievsky2025intent} proposed an alternative decomposition that separates intent uncertainty, articulation variance, and model uncertainty, but this framework does not cross infrastructure and methodology facets in a factorial design. Our work extends these approaches by crossing seven facets across five benchmarks, enabling direct comparison of variance structures across evaluation formats.

\paragraph{Infrastructure and Prompt Variance.}
A parallel line of work has examined individual variance sources in isolation. On the infrastructure side, \citet{yuan2025layercast} documented up to 9\% accuracy variation from numerical precision and GPU type but provided no factorial attribution. \citet{messina2026background} formalized the concept of background temperature from floating-point non-associativity in GPU computation, and \citet{thinkingmachines2025batch} proposed batch-invariant kernels to mitigate CUDA nondeterminism. On the methodology side, \citet{sclar2024formatting} found up to 76\% accuracy swings from prompt formatting changes on fixed hardware, and BrittleBench \citep{brittlebench2026} reported 50\% variance from prompt perturbation. \citet{pezeshkpour2024ordering} showed that answer ordering in multiple-choice benchmarks introduces systematic bias, while \citet{bui2025seeds} and \citet{biderman2024lessons} documented sensitivity to random seeds and training configurations. Each of these studies isolates one factor while holding others fixed, which precludes estimating interactions between sources or comparing their relative magnitudes within a unified design.

\paragraph{IRT and Efficient Benchmarking.}
Item Response Theory (IRT) offers a complementary perspective on evaluation design by modeling item difficulty and discrimination parameters. \citet{lalor2019irt} introduced IRT-based item analysis for NLP benchmarks, and \citet{rodriguez2021leaderboards} showed that IRT reveals hidden properties of evaluation items that raw accuracy scores obscure. Building on this foundation, \citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} developed tinyBenchmarks, using IRT-based subsampling to reduce evaluation cost while preserving ranking accuracy, and \citet{zhou2026irt} recommended a 116-item subset via PSN-IRT for efficient MMLU evaluation. \citet{perlitz2023efficient} explored related strategies for benchmark compression. These approaches optimize item selection for latent trait estimation but do not model variance from prompt, seed, or ordering facets. IRT-selected items evaluated under a single methodological condition can therefore systematically underestimate the number of items required for reliable measurement across diverse evaluation settings.

\paragraph{Binary Data and Psychometrics Foundations.}
Our application of G-theory to binary correctness scores draws on established psychometric methodology. \citet{shavelson1991generalizability} and \citet{brennan2001generalizability} provide the canonical framework for multi-facet reliability analysis. \citet{mushquash2006gtheory} extended G-theory to binary data, and \citet{jiang2018bayesian} developed logistic-link generalized G-theory for binary outcomes. \citet{tuerlinckx2006glmm} established the connection between generalized linear mixed models and G-theory variance components. These methods ground our use of Henderson Method~I for binary correctness scores (\S\ref{sec:binary}).

\paragraph{Positioning.}
Relative to \citet{mesting2026hidden}, we fix the judge (generation-based scoring) to isolate the remaining facets; their judge-dominated variance (43.8\%) would otherwise mask all other sources. Our multi-benchmark design reveals format-conditional structure that single-benchmark studies cannot detect. Relative to IRT approaches \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks, zhou2026irt}, G-theory and IRT optimize orthogonal dimensions of evaluation design: IRT selects informative items, while G-theory determines the conditions needed for a target reliability. Our D-study quantifies this complementarity: multi-facet designs require 10.0$\times$ fewer items than single-condition protocols for equivalent reliability. Relative to isolated-factor studies \citep{yuan2025layercast, sclar2024formatting, brittlebench2026}, our design crosses infrastructure and methodology facets within a single factorial framework, enabling interaction estimation that per-factor studies cannot provide.

\end{table}

The variance structure is format-conditional: MC benchmarks concentrate variance in items and model$\times$item interaction (34--38\% and 30--36\%), while FF benchmarks distribute it across item, model, and interaction components (5--24\% each) with larger residuals (40--44\%). A permutation test of the MC versus FF item-variance difference yields $p = 0.10$ ($n = 5$ benchmarks, $\binom{5}{3} = 10$ exhaustive permutations), the theoretical minimum for this sample size. The effect size is large (Cohen's $d = 1.73$; mean difference 95\% CI [11.44, 21.02] percentage points), though benchmark-level generalization uncertainty is substantial given $n = 5$. This structural difference does not extend to item budgets: GSM8K requires 128 items for $G \geq 0.80$---the highest of all benchmarks---while HellaSwag (MC, 118) exceeds both ARC (MC, 98) and MATH (FF, 101), demonstrating that format alone does not predict evaluation cost. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes the contrast.

To probe the mechanism behind item variance, we exploit the natural heterogeneity within MMLU's 57 subjects. Subjects with higher item difficulty dispersion (measured by $\text{std}(p_i)$ across models) exhibit higher item variance shares, with Spearman $\rho = 0.806$ ($p = 0.005$, $n = 10$ subjects). Notably, \texttt{high\_school\_mathematics}, the only MC subject with open-ended numerical items, shows item variance of just 10.4\%---below the free-form benchmark GSM8K (16.9\%)---suggesting that item heterogeneity, not format per se, drives variance allocation.

